      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Fuzzy Edit Processing Flow\n├── Main edit loop \u003c-- 2a\n│   ├── applyEdit() function \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:152\n│   │   ├── Switch on match mode \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:153\n│   │   └── applyFuzzyMatch() \u003c-- 2b\n│   │       ├── findFuzzyCandidates() \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:309\n│   │       │   ├── Build candidate list \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:314\n│   │       │   └── Score each candidate \u003c-- 2c\n│   │       └── Apply replacements \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:230\n│   │           ├── Global mode \u003c-- 2d\n│   │           └── Single best match \u003c-- skills/fs_apply_edit/main.go:244\n│   └── File operation with backup \u003c-- 2e",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen AI agents need to edit code, they often have **approximate knowledge** of where changes should be made. They might know \"around line 45\" or \"near the function that handles requests\" but not the exact text. The fuzzy edit system solves this by using **contextual clues** like line hints, surrounding code, and similarity scoring to find the right location even when the search pattern isn't exact.\n\n## Details\n\nThe system processes edits through a **scoring algorithm** that evaluates potential matches [2c]. Each candidate gets points for word overlap, proximity to the suggested line hint, and matching context before/after the target. The **global vs single** logic determines whether to replace all matches or just the best-scoring one [2d]. Before any changes are applied, the original content is automatically backed up to **CAS storage** for undo capability [2e]. The fuzzy matching handles three modes: exact text match, regex patterns, and the default fuzzy mode that tolerates whitespace and minor variations while still being precise enough to avoid accidental changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "title": "Diff Generation and Analysis",
