        },
        {
          "id": "3c",
          "path": "skills/code_diff/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 224,
          "lineContent": "changes = append(changes, struct{ op, oldPos, newPos int }{1, i, j - 1})",
          "title": "Change Tracking",
          "description": "Tracks additions, deletions, and common lines during backtrack"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "skills/code_diff/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 112,
          "lineContent": "result.Unified = generateUnifiedDiff(result.OldFile, result.NewFile, hunks)",
          "title": "Unified Diff Format",
          "description": "Generates git-style unified diff output"
        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "skills/code_diff/main.go",
          "lineNumber": 119,
          "lineContent": "artifact, err = skillmain.PersistBuffer(ctx, rc, buf, \"text/plain\", \"code_diff\")",
          "title": "Large Diff Handling",
          "description": "Stores large diffs in CAS with truncation for display"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Diff Generation Pipeline\n├── skill entry point (code/diff) \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:51\n│   ├── run() function \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:55\n│   │   ├── read files \u0026 normalize \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:77\n│   │   │   ├── computeDiff() call \u003c-- 3a\n│   │   │   │   ├── longestCommonSubsequence() \u003c-- 3b\n│   │   │   │   │   └── backtrack \u0026 track changes \u003c-- 3c\n│   │   │   │   └── buildHunks() from changes \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:207\n│   │   │   └── computeStats() from hunks \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:100\n│   │   └── generateUnifiedDiff() output \u003c-- 3d\n│   └── handle large diff artifact \u003c-- 3e\n└── LCS Algorithm Core\n    ├── dynamic programming table \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:187\n    └── backtrack to find changes \u003c-- skills/code_diff/main.go:216",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nThe diff generation system solves the problem of **showing precise changes between file versions** in a structured, machine-readable format. When developers need to understand what changed between two files, they need more than just a visual diff - they need structured data that can be analyzed, processed, and applied programmatically. This system provides **unified diffs with statistics, change tracking, and artifact storage** for large diffs, enabling both human review and automated processing.\n\n# Details\n\nThe diff system uses a **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) algorithm** to find the minimal set of changes between files [3b]. The process begins by reading both files and normalizing whitespace if requested [3a]. The algorithm builds a dynamic programming table to track common lines, then backtracks through this table to identify additions, deletions, and unchanged sections [3c].\n\nThese changes are organized into **hunks** - contiguous blocks of changes with context lines [3a]. Each hunk contains line-by-line differences with prefixes (`+` for additions, `-` for deletions, ` ` for context). The system also computes statistics like lines added/removed and similarity percentages.\n\nFor large diffs that exceed display limits, the system **automatically stores the full diff in CAS** (Content Addressable Storage) and provides a truncated preview with retrieval hints [3e]. This ensures the output remains manageable while preserving full fidelity for those who need it.\n\nThe final output is a **structured JSON envelope** containing the unified diff text, parsed hunks, statistics, and optional CAS artifact reference [3d]. This structured format enables downstream tools to parse, analyze, and even apply the diffs programmatically."
    },
