      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Agent Tools Diff Application\n├── applyStructuredDiff() entry point \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/edit_tools.go:233\n│   ├── Parse diff JSON from code/diff \u003c-- 5a\n│   ├── Apply hunks in reverse order \u003c-- 5b\n│   │   └── applyHunk() for each hunk \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/edit_tools.go:456\n│   │       ├── Validate context lines \u003c-- 5d\n│   │       └── Build new file content \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/edit_tools.go:533\n│   └── Atomic file update \u003c-- 5e\n└── Error handling \u0026 validation \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/edit_tools.go:290\n    ├── Context mismatch detection \u003c-- 5d\n    └── File write error handling \u003c-- internal/agent/tools/edit_tools.go:320",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen AI agents need to edit files, they face a critical challenge: how to apply precise changes without corrupting files or losing work. The **structured diff application system** solves this by providing a reliable way to apply code changes generated by the `code/diff` skill back to actual files. Instead of risky text replacements, agents can generate a structured diff, validate it, and apply it atomically with full rollback capability.\n\n## Details\n\nThe system works by parsing structured JSON diffs from the `code/diff` skill [5a], which contain hunks with line-by-line change instructions. These hunks are applied **in reverse order** [5b] to preserve line numbering accuracy - a crucial detail that prevents cascading offset errors. Each hunk undergoes **context validation** [5d] to ensure the file hasn't changed since the diff was generated, preventing accidental corruption. The system builds the new file content by stitching together unchanged context with additions and deletions [5e], then performs an **atomic file update** using truncate-and-rewrite to prevent partial writes. If any validation fails or context mismatches are detected, the entire operation aborts safely, leaving the original file untouched.\n\nThis approach gives agents the power of sophisticated diff-based editing while maintaining the safety guarantees needed for production code manipulation."
    }
  ],
